    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term",
    "problem": "The coefficient of ${x}^{22}$ in the expansion of ${(3+{x}^{2})}^{13}$ is",
    "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3+{x}^{2})}^{13},$ we have $a=3,$ $b={x}^{2},$ and $n=13.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+{x}^{2})}^{13}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{0}){3}^{13}{({x}^{2})}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{1}){3}^{13-1}{({x}^{2})}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{2}){3}^{13-2}{({x}^{2})}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{13}){3}^{13-13}{({x}^{2})}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{22}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{11}){3}^{13-11}{({x}^{2})}^{11}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{11}){3}^{13-11}{({x}^{2})}^{11} & =78\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{22} \\\\  & =78\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{22} \\\\  & =702{x}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{22}$ is $702.$",
